# ─── DenchClaw CRM — the crank ───────────────────────────────────────────────
# Install as the user that owns the app (yogi), NOT root:
#
#     crontab -e -u yogi          # paste the block below
#     crontab -l -u yogi          # confirm
#
# Until something here is installed, the CRM looks completely healthy and sends
# nothing: the executors are one-batch-per-call by design, so an uninstalled
# cron is indistinguishable from an empty queue. `node bin/preflight.mjs` calls
# that state out explicitly.

# Mail failures somewhere a human reads. bin/tick.mjs exits non-zero only on a
# real failure — a blocked channel or an unconnected Tantra tenant is a normal
# 200 and stays silent, so this will not cry wolf.
MAILTO=ops@usetantra.com
PATH=/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin

# Sends: every 2 minutes. The interval is a latency choice, not a throughput
# one — each run drains a batch per channel, and the advisory lock inside
# bin/tick.mjs means a slow run is skipped rather than stacked.
# TANTRA_TICK_ENABLED=0 here so the mirror is driven by exactly ONE schedule
# (the 5-minute job below) rather than by both.
*/2 * * * * cd /home/yogi/denchclaw-crm && TANTRA_TICK_ENABLED=0 /usr/bin/node bin/tick.mjs >> /var/log/denchclaw-tick.log 2>&1

# Tantra mirror: every 5 minutes, separately from sends.
# The mirror is POLL-driven by design (their webhooks are reply-shaped and would
# silently miss our own sends, second inbounds and new threads), so this cron IS
# the mirror — not a backstop for it.
*/5 * * * * cd /home/yogi/denchclaw-crm && /usr/bin/node bin/tick.mjs --channels= >> /var/log/denchclaw-tick.log 2>&1

# Preflight once a day: catches the slow failures — cron silently dying, a
# migration applied on one box and not another, a key rotated out from under
# stored credentials. Mails only when something is BLOCKING (exit 1).
30 7 * * * cd /home/yogi/denchclaw-crm && /usr/bin/node bin/preflight.mjs > /tmp/denchclaw-preflight.txt 2>&1 || cat /tmp/denchclaw-preflight.txt

# Backups: nightly, verified once a week. --verify actually restores the dump
# into a scratch database and counts tables, because an unverified backup is a
# hope rather than a backup.
# NOTE: this writes to LOCAL DISK. Ship BACKUP_DIR off the box as well, or it
# does not survive losing the box.
15 2 * * 1-6 cd /home/yogi/denchclaw-crm && bin/backup.sh >> /var/log/denchclaw-backup.log 2>&1
15 2 * * 0   cd /home/yogi/denchclaw-crm && bin/backup.sh --verify >> /var/log/denchclaw-backup.log 2>&1

# Log rotation — /var/log/denchclaw-tick.log grows forever otherwise. Prefer a
# real /etc/logrotate.d entry; this is the zero-dependency version.
0 4 * * 0 : > /var/log/denchclaw-tick.log

# ── Interval guidance ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Every run costs one loopback request per (tenant × enabled channel). With one
# tenant and four channels that is trivial. Before onboarding many tenants,
# either raise the interval or shard by tenant with --tenant=, because the
# script walks them serially inside a single lock.
#
# Do NOT run this on two boxes at once against the same database expecting the
# lock to sort it out — it will (the lock is a Postgres advisory lock, so it is
# genuinely cluster-wide), but the losing box does no work, which makes the
# second box pure cost.
